 for bestpractices and # Create Content

## Overview
Develop a holistic Create Content experience for LifeOps: an AI-assisted content creation editor that enables drafting, research, versioning, scheduling, and collaboration. This page sits at the center of the Content Pipeline Module (idea generation → research → drafting → editing → scheduling → publishing) and is tightly integrated with the Scoped Shared Memory & Vector DB, as well as Storage & Artifacts. The prompt should yield a production-ready implementation with robust runtime safety (null/undefined guards, proper state defaults, and validated API responses) and a cohesive, dark-mode-first UI that aligns with the LifeOps design system.

## Page Description (Full Detail)
What this page is:
- Create Content is the primary content creation canvas. It provides an AI-assisted drafting environment, a research pane, versioning controls, and scheduling options for multi-platform publishing. It also interfaces with an end-to-end Content Pipeline to automate idea generation, research collection, drafting, and editing, then pushes to publishing pipelines.

Goals:
- Enable rapid, traceable content creation with AI suggestions, inline research citations, and structured outline generation.
- Offer robust version history, diffs, and rollback capabilities.
- Provide scheduling controls to publish to multiple platforms at configurable times and time zones.
- Maintain a secure, auditable trail of actions, artifacts, and inter-agent memory scoped to the current content item.
- Ensure data integrity and safety with validation, access controls, and reversible actions.

Connected features and APIs:
- Content Pipeline Module: Orchestrates idea generation, research retrieval, drafting, editing, and multi-platform publishing with prompts/templates.
- Scoped Shared Memory & Vector DB: Persistent, scoped memory for agents; supports read/write with TTLs and access controls; uses a vector DB for retrieval and a relational store for metadata.
- Storage & Artifacts: Secure file storage for run artifacts, diffs, generated content, and logs; supports signed URLs and retention policies.

UI elements and visual guidance:
- Rich text editor supporting markdown, real-time collaboration cursors, and AI-assisted drafting prompts.
- AI assistant sidebar for research, outlines, prompts, and suggested rewrites.
- Research pane with source citations, relevance scoring, and retrieval controls.
- Version history panel with diffs, compare view, and rollback capabilities.
- Scheduling controls with per-platform toggles, run times, time zone selection, and recurrence options.
- Auto-save, draft list, and draft lifecycle status indicators.
- Activity log and an inter-agent trace viewer for auditability.
- Clear visual hierarchy with cards, sections, spaces, and consistent typography.

Visual guidance:
- Dark-mode-first, flat color blocks with layered cards and subtle elevation.
- Cards with rounded corners, thin borders, hover states, and bottom-anchored actions.
- Sidebar navigation with collapsible sections for Projects, Support, Apps, and active route highlighting.
- Typography emphasizes headings, section titles, and readable body text.

Connected pages and features:
- Edit Content: Access an existing item for editing with AI suggestions, review comments, and workflow state transitions.
- Content List / Library: Browse, search, and filter content drafts, published items, and templates.

API integrations:
- No external APIs required for core generation in this prompt. The implementation should be prepared to connect to internal LifeOps services (Content Pipeline, Vector DB, Storage) via defined internal APIs.

## Components to Build
1) CreateContentEditor (Main Canvas)
- Rich text editor with markdown support, collaboration cursors, autosave, and AI-assisted drafting prompts.
- AI Assistant sidebar: research prompts, outline generation, clause rewrites, and topic suggestions.
- Inline citation manager and bibliography view.
- Versioning panel: shows versions, diffs, and one-click restore.
- Scheduling section: per-platform publish toggles, times, time zones, and recurrence.
- Draft list widget and autosave status indicators.
- Interaction with Content Pipeline for generation and editing flows.
- State management: local UI state plus global content state.

2) ResearchPane
- Source retrieval and relevance scoring UI.
- Research note capture tied to content item.
- Filter by topic, source type, date, confidence.

3) VersionControlPanel
- Version timeline, diffs, compare view, and restore actions.
- Diff rendering highlights added/removed content between versions.
- Link artifacts to run IDs and memory contexts.

4) SchedulingPanel
- Platform publish toggles (e.g., Blog, Social, Newsletter).
- Schedule picker with cron-like UI or human-friendly builder.
- Timezone selection, recurrence rules, and max retries.
- Safety rails: approvals for scheduled actions if required.

5) MemoryInspector (Scoped Shared Memory)
- View and modify scoped memory with access controls.
- TTL management, read/write permissions, and memory scope filters.

6) VectorMemoryBridge
- Abstraction layer to read/write vectorized memories to vector DB and metadata to relational store.
- Validation, access controls, and TTL enforcement.

7) ArtifactsStoragePanel
- Display run artifacts, generated content diffs, logs.
- Signed URL generation, retention policies, and download actions.

8) ContentLibraryList
- Browse, search, and filter existing content drafts, published items, and templates.
- Quick actions: open in editor, duplicate, archive, or delete (with safety checks).

9) AuditTrailPanel
- Show action logs, agent communications, prompts used, and diffs for each action.

10) PublishGateway
- Multi-platform publish runner orchestrator (invoked by scheduling and manual publish).
- Capture run history, logs, and errors; feed back into Content history.

11) API Gateways and Data Models
- Define REST/GraphQL endpoints for content CRUD, versioning, scheduling, memory read/write, and artifact access.
- Data model adapters for ContentItem, ContentVersion, Schedule, MemoryScope, RunArtifact, and PlatformPublishRecord.

## Implementation Requirements

### Frontend
- Framework: React with a robust state management approach (e.g., React Query or SWR for data fetching, Zustand or Redux for UI state).
- Type safety: TypeScript with strict types for all data models.
- State defaults and guards:
  - useState<T[]>([]) for arrays.
  - data ?? [] pattern when consuming Supabase-like query results.
  - Array.isArray checks before .map/.filter/.reduce.
- Components must guard against null/undefined values when rendering lists or mapping data.
- Accessibility: ARIA roles, labeled controls, keyboard navigation, and high-contrast focus outlines.
- Theming: Centralized theme tokens to map to the provided color palette; ensure dark-mode primary surfaces and card elevations.
- Data safety: All API responses validated; use optional chaining; destructuring with defaults.
- Collaboration: Real-time editing surfaces with cursors and presence indicators (mock or integrated if a real collaboration backend is available).
- Performance: Debounced AI prompts, lazy-loading of tenants (content items), and memoization for heavy render paths.
- Testing: Unit tests for data transformations, integration tests for scheduling logic, and end-to-end tests for create/edit flows.

### Backend
- Endpoints (examples to implement):
  - POST /api/content -> create content item
  - GET /api/content?filters... -> list content items with pagination
  - GET /api/content/:id -> fetch content item with versions
  - POST /api/content/:id/versions -> create a new version (diffs captured)
  - POST /api/content/:id/publish -> trigger publishing to platforms
  - GET /api/content/:id/memory -> read scoped memory
  - POST /api/content/:id/memory -> write scoped memory with TTL
  - GET /api/content/:id/artifacts -> fetch artifacts
  - GET /api/content/:id/audit -> fetch audit trail
  - POST /api/schedules -> create schedule
  - GET /api/schedules/:id/run -> fetch run history
- Database schema highlights:
  - ContentItem(id, title, slug, status, createdAt, updatedAt, currentPlatformState, ...)
  - ContentVersion(id, contentItemId, versionNumber, changes, authorId, createdAt, isDiff)
  - Schedule(id, name, enabled, cronExpression, timezone, targetContentId, inputPayload, permissions, constraints, safetyRails, retryPolicy, createdAt, updatedAt)
  - MemoryScope(id, contentItemId, scopeName, dataBlob, ttl, accessControls, createdAt, updatedAt)
  - RunArtifact(id, contentItemId, runId, type, path, signedUrl, retentionPolicy, createdAt)
  - AuditLog(id, contentItemId, action, actorId, timestamp, details)
  - PlatformPublishRecord(id, contentItemId, platform, status, scheduledAt, startedAt, finishedAt, logs)
- Vector DB integration:
  - Upsert and query memory vectors by scope with TTL logic and access control checks.
  - Memory can be read by agents only within allowed scopes.
- Storage & Artifacts:
  - Secure file storage with signed URL generation (e.g., via a storage service) and retention policies.

### Integration
- Frontend ↔ Backend: Use REST/GraphQL with standardized response shapes; consistently apply data validation and typing.
- Memory ↔ Agents: The Create Content flow reads/writes to scoped memory via the MemoryBridge; ensure TTLs and permissions are enforced.
- Content Pipeline: The editor triggers ideas, research, and drafting steps via the pipeline; the pipeline writes versioned content and artifacts back to the content item.
- Scheduling: Scheduling panel configures cron-like jobs; a Cronjobs service executes and pushes content to platforms via the PublishGateway.

## User Experience Flow
1) User opens Create Content.
2) Editor loads with an empty draft or an existing draft loaded from Content Library.
3) User uses AI Assistant to generate an outline or ideas; AI prompts provide draft segments.
4) User edits content in Rich Text Editor; autosave occurs in background; memory is updated with relevant notes.
5) User taps Research pane to pull in citations and source summaries; memory entries are created for agents.
6) User reviews version history; compares or restores previous versions as needed.
7) Scheduling panel: User toggles publishing per platform, selects times/time zones, and defines recurrence. Approvals workflow triggers if required.
8) Publish: When scheduled or manually triggered, PublishGateway runs the multi-platform publish, logs outputs, and stores artifacts.
9) Artifacts and diffs are accessible via ArtifactsStoragePanel; signed URLs grant access with retention policies.
10) All actions are captured in an AuditTrailPanel for traceability.

## Technical Specifications

Data Models: (illustrative schemas; adapt to your ORM)
- ContentItem
  - id: string
  - title: string
  - slug: string
  - status: 'draft'|'review'|'scheduled'|'published'
  - currentVersionId: string
  - createdAt: string
  - updatedAt: string
  - authorId: string
  - memoryScopeIds: string[]
  - platformPublishConfig: any
- ContentVersion
  - id: string
  - contentItemId: string
  - versionNumber: number
  - changes: string // diff or patch data
  - authorId: string
  - createdAt: string
  - isDiff: boolean
- Schedule
  - id: string
  - name: string
  - enabled: boolean
  - cronExpression: string
  - timezone: string
  - targetContentId: string
  - inputPayload: any
  - permissions: 'suggest-only'|'approval-required'|'conditional-auto'|'bounded-autopilot'
  - constraints: { maxActions?: number; spendLimit?: number; allowedTools?: string[] }
  - safetyRails: string[]
  - retryPolicy: { backoffMs: number; maxRetries: number; deadLetterQueue?: string }
  - createdAt/updatedAt
- MemoryScope
  - id: string
  - contentItemId: string
  - scopeName: string
  - dataBlob: any
  - ttlSeconds: number
  - accessControls: any
  - createdAt/updatedAt
- RunArtifact
  - id: string
  - contentItemId: string
  - runId: string
  - type: 'diff'|'generatedContent'|'log'|'artifact'
  - path: string
  - signedUrl: string
  - retentionPolicy: string
  - createdAt
- AuditLog
  - id: string
  - contentItemId: string
  - action: string
  - actorId: string
  - timestamp: string
  - details: any
- PlatformPublishRecord
  - id: string
  - contentItemId: string
  - platform: string
  - status: 'queued'|'in-progress'|'completed'|'failed'
  - scheduledAt: string
  - startedAt: string
  - finishedAt: string
  - logs: string

API Endpoints (example set):
- GET /api/content
- POST /api/content
- GET /api/content/:id
- PUT /api/content/:id
- DELETE /api/content/:id
- POST /api/content/:id/versions
- GET /api/content/:id/versions
- POST /api/content/:id/publish
- GET /api/content/:id/memory
- POST /api/content/:id/memory
- GET /api/content/:id/artifacts
- GET /api/content/:id/audit
- POST /api/schedules
- GET /api/schedules/:id/run

Security:
- Auth: JWT/OAuth2; enforce RBAC: roles like Editor, Approver, Admin.
- Access controls on MemoryScope and content actions; scoped memory cannot be accessed outside allowed contexts.
- All mutations require proper validation and permission checks.
- Signed URLs for artifacts with short lifetimes; retention policies enforced at the storage layer.

Validation:
- All API responses validated; use data?.field or defaults; guard arrays with (array ?? []) or Array.isArray(array) ? array.map(...) : [].
- On frontend data loading: const items = data ?? [] ; Array.isArray(items) guard usage.

Validation Rules:
- Inputs: title must be non-empty; content length >= 1; schedule cron expression validated; time zone valid; platform list non-empty if publishing toggled on.
- Versioning: every save creates a new ContentVersion; currentVersionId must reference an existing version.
- Memory: TTL > 0 when set; accessControls not empty for restricted scopes.
- Artifacts: path and signedUrl must be non-empty when created.

Acceptance Criteria
- [ ] Create Content editor loads with a draft or empty state and autosaves without errors; data is guarded against null values (no runtime crashes).
- [ ] AI-assisted drafting prompts populate accurately; research pane shows relevant citations with ability to add inline notes; all array-like data is guarded using (data ?? []) or Array.isArray checks.
- [ ] Versioning works: create version, view diffs, compare view, and restore to previous version without data loss; correct diffs displayed.
- [ ] Scheduling supports per-platform toggles, time zones, and recurrence; scheduled runs execute and publish to platforms; run logs are captured.
- [ ] Memory API correctly reads/writes scoped memory with TTL and access controls; no unauthorized reads.
- [ ] Artifacts are stored securely with signed URLs and retention policies; downloads succeed and expire correctly.
- [ ] Content Library integration: Create Content can be opened from library, edited, and saved back with versioning and artifacts updated.
- [ ] Access controls enforced: unauthorized actions are blocked; audit trail captures all actions.

UI/UX Guidelines
- Apply LifeOps design system:
  - Card-based modular layout with 12-column grid and 24–32px gutters.
  - Sidebar navigation with active state indicators; top bar with search and quick actions.
  - Dark background, high-contrast typography, and accent colors from the palette.
  - Interactive elements: rounded buttons, large input fields with soft borders, focus outlines in accent color.
  - Accessibility: keyboard navigable, aria-labels, screen-reader friendly.

Visual Style
- As provided: deep charcoal surfaces, flat color blocks, neon accents for charts if used, but primarily flat UI with depth via elevation.
- Use the exact palette tokens wherever possible:
  - Primary background: #18191C
  - Card surfaces: #232429 or #1F2124
  - Accent: #4F8CFF
  - Success: #5ED36D
  - Warning/Info: #FFD66C
  - Error: #EF6464
  - Text: #FFFFFF (headings), #AEB2B8 (secondary), #74777D (placeholders)
  - Borders: rgba(38,40,44,0.3) to rgba(38,40,44,0.6)

Mandatory Coding Standards — Runtime Safety
- Supabase-like results handling:
  - const items = data ?? [];
- Array methods:
  - (items ?? []).map(...)
  - Array.isArray(items) ? items.map(...) : []
- useState defaults for arrays:
  - const [items, setItems] = useState<Type[]>([]);
- API response shapes:
  - const list = Array.isArray(response?.data) ? response.data : [];
- Optional chaining:
  - obj?.property?.nested
- Destructuring with defaults:
  - const { items = [], count = 0 } = response ?? {};

Development Prompts for AI Tool
- Generate a fully-typed React + TypeScript frontend module for Create Content that includes:
  - Editor area with markdown support, collaboration cursors, autosave, and connectable to Content Pipeline for drafting prompts.
  - AI Assistant sidebar with sections for Outline, Ideas, and Rewrites; each section should fetch prompts and maintain local state with safe guards.
  - ResearchPane showing citations and source metadata with relevance scores; allow tagging/categorization.
  - Versioning/DiffViewer that fetches ContentVersion history and renders diffs with a side-by-side view.
  - SchedulingPanel that validates cron or UI-based scheduling, handles time zone, and supports multiple platforms with platform toggles.
  - MemoryInspector and MemoryBridge implementing scoped memory read/write with TTL and ACLs; safely handle nulls.
  - ArtifactsStoragePanel to display artifacts with signed URLs and retention status; implement safe rendering for possibly empty lists.
  - ContentLibraryList to browse and filter content items; ensure guard clauses on list rendering.
  - AuditTrailPanel to display action logs and inter-agent communications; ensure privacy controls.
- Implement data models and API hooks:
  - TypeScript interfaces for ContentItem, ContentVersion, Schedule, MemoryScope, RunArtifact, AuditLog, PlatformPublishRecord.
  - Custom React hooks for data fetching with null-safe handling: useQueryContent, useContentVersions, useMemoryScoper, useArtifacts, useSchedules, etc.
  - API client wrappers with proper payload validation and response guards.
- State management:
  - Use React Query or SWR for data fetching; use useState/useEffect for local UI state with proper initialization (e.g., [] defaults).
  - Ensure mutation hooks validate input and enforce optimistic updates with rollback on error.
- Security and permissions:
  - Guard all mutations with authorization checks; memory read/write respects scope ACLs.
  - Include audit trail capture for UI actions; ensure sensitive fields are redacted when displayed.
- Accessibility:
  - aria-labels, keyboard shortcuts for common actions, and proper focus management.
- Testing:
  - Unit tests for data transformers and reducers; integration tests for memory read/write flows; e2e tests for create/edit/publish flows.

Notes and Assumptions
- The prompt should be capable of generating a complete, production-ready module with the above components.
- If certain features require placeholder implementations (e.g., collaboration cursors or external AI calls), provide clear stubs with extensible interfaces and default behavior that satisfies runtime safety and UI expectations.
- Do not assume an external API is available; design with clear extension points for internal services (Content Pipeline, Vector DB, Storage).

This prompt is ready to be consumed by an AI development tool to produce a complete, end-to-end Create Content feature set that adheres to the project’s architectural patterns, UI guidelines, and runtime safety requirements. for style.

CRITICAL DESIGN REQUIREMENTS:
- You MUST follow ALL design principles and patterns specified in the Design Reference above
- Do NOT deviate from the design specifications without explicit user permission
- Implement ALL visual design elements (colors, typography, spacing, shadows, animations) as specified
- Follow the layout patterns and component designs exactly as documented
- Ensure all interactions and micro-animations match the reference specifications

CRITICAL TECHNICAL REQUIREMENTS:
- React 18.3.1, React Router 6.30.1
- Tailwind CSS v3 with tailwind.config.js and custom keyframes
- @tailwindcss/typography plugin for rich text content
- Tailwind CSS animations (NOT Motion library or framer-motion)
- Sonner for toasts
- Recharts for data visualization
- Native fetch() with API utilities in src/lib/api.ts
- Design system with CSS custom properties
- Use RGB color values for theming
- When Supabase is configured: use Edge Functions for LLM and server-only logic; never expose API keys in the client

